<h1 data-hero-anim="2" style="margin:0;font-family:var(--font-display);font-weight:400;font-size:clamp(40px,5.6vw,76px);line-height:1;letter-spacing:-.02em;max-width:16ch">Stem Cell Therapy for <span style="color:var(--sand-500);font-style:italic">Knee Osteoarthritis</span></h1>
<p data-hero-anim="3" style="margin:28px 0 0;max-width:56ch;font-family:var(--font-body);font-size:clamp(17px,1.6vw,20px);line-height:1.6;color:var(--text-on-dark-secondary)">Considering regenerative treatment for knee arthritis? The biggest decision is not whether to “get stem cells.” It is understanding what treatment is actually being proposed, whether the evidence supports it for your knee, and whether the clinic is worth trusting.</p>
<div data-hero-anim="4" style="margin:28px 0 0;max-width:60ch;padding:2px 0 2px 22px;border-left:1px solid var(--sand-500);font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--text-on-dark-secondary)">Some cell-based treatments may improve pain and function in selected people with knee osteoarthritis, but evidence remains mixed and product-dependent. Routine injections have not been shown to reliably rebuild normal cartilage or reverse osteoarthritis. This guide helps you compare the evidence, treatment types, candidacy, safety, costs and provider quality before you commit.<a href="#ref-1" style="font-size:.72em;vertical-align:super;margin-left:2px;color:var(--sand-500)">1</a><a href="#ref-2" style="font-size:.72em;vertical-align:super;margin-left:2px;color:var(--sand-500)">2</a></div>
